Rem ==================================================================
Rem eng.shoddy — test program for machines/eng.shoddy
Rem
Rem Assert-driven, in the csvtest.shoddy / fin.shoddy pattern: it prints
Rem what it is doing, stops at the first failed assertion, and ends with
Rem ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED when none fail.
Rem
Rem Every number here is a KNOWN ANSWER — a textbook figure, an identity,
Rem or one worked by hand — never a value recorded from a run. A numerics
Rem library whose tests record its own output tests nothing at all.
Rem
Rem This file can only see Eng* words and builtins. eng.shoddy includes
Rem stats, math, seq and str, and since v1.6.0 those do not bleed through
Rem to a consumer, so Mean and StartsWith are not in scope here. That is
Rem the scoping working, and it makes this file a live test of it: if the
Rem wrapped surface were incomplete, this program would not compile.
Rem
Rem The ones that matter most are easy to miss:
Rem
Rem   * EngComb(52, 5) must be 2598960 EXACTLY. The factorial form
Rem     overflows past 2^53 on the way, so an exact answer is what proves
Rem     the multiplicative form is the one that ran.
Rem   * EngStdDev and EngStdDevP are checked on the SAME data. They differ
Rem     by the n-versus-n-1 divisor and nothing else, so a test that used
Rem     one figure would pass with either word wired to either divisor.
Rem   * EngCubicReal is checked on both branches. The three-real-root case
Rem     goes through the trigonometric form and the single-root case
Rem     through Cardano's radicals; they share no arithmetic.
Rem   * EngNormalize is checked on a flat list, where the range is zero
Rem     and the obvious implementation divides by it.
Rem
Rem What cannot be tested here: the aborting half of every guard. Shoddy
Rem has no catchable errors, so EngZeroOf's refusal on a bracket with no
Rem sign change, EngConvert's on two different quantities, EngAcosh's on
Rem x < 1, EngIntegrate's on an odd n, EngModInv's on a non-coprime pair
Rem and EngPolyDiv's on a zero divisor are verified by hand and named
Rem here instead.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../machines/eng.shoddy"

Rem Doubles compare to a tolerance. The answers that must be exact are
Rem asserted with = and would otherwise hide a real defect behind one.
Def Close(a As Number, b As Number, tol As Number) As Boolean
    Abs(a - b) <= tol

Def Main()
    Print("--- ANGLES ---")
    Assert(Close(EngRad(180), Pi(), 0.0000000001), "180 DEGREES IS PI RADIANS")
    Assert(Close(EngDeg(Pi()), 180, 0.0000000001), "AND BACK AGAIN")
    Assert(Close(EngGradOf(Pi() / 2), 100, 0.0000000001), "A RIGHT ANGLE IS 100 GRADS")
    Assert(Close(EngFromGrad(100), Pi() / 2, 0.0000000001), "AND BACK AGAIN")
    Rem 30.5 degrees is 30 degrees and 30 minutes exactly.
    Assert(Degs(EngDmsOf(30.5)) = 30, "DMS DEGREES")
    Assert(Mins(EngDmsOf(30.5)) = 30, "DMS MINUTES")
    Assert(Close(Secs(EngDmsOf(30.5)), 0, 0.0000001), "DMS SECONDS")
    Assert(Close(EngFromDms(EngDmsOf(30.5)), 30.5, 0.0000000001), "DMS ROUND TRIP")
    Rem The sign lives on the degrees and must survive the trip back.
    Assert(Close(EngFromDms(EngDmsOf(0 - 30.5)), 0 - 30.5, 0.0000000001),
           "AND A NEGATIVE BEARING ROUND TRIPS")

    Print("--- TRIGONOMETRY ---")
    Assert(Close(EngSec(0), 1, 0.0000000001), "SECANT OF ZERO")
    Assert(Close(EngCsc(Pi() / 2), 1, 0.0000000001), "COSECANT OF A RIGHT ANGLE")
    Assert(Close(EngCot(Pi() / 4), 1, 0.0000000001), "COTANGENT OF 45 DEGREES")
    Assert(Close(EngAsec(2), Pi() / 3, 0.0000000001), "ARCSECANT OF 2 IS 60 DEGREES")
    Assert(Close(EngAcsc(2), Pi() / 6, 0.0000000001), "ARCCOSECANT OF 2 IS 30 DEGREES")
    Assert(Close(EngAcot(1), Pi() / 4, 0.0000000001), "ARCCOTANGENT OF 1")
    Assert(Close(EngHypot(3, 4), 5, 0.0000000001), "THE 3-4-5 TRIANGLE")
    Assert(Close(EngDistOf(0, 0, 3, 4), 5, 0.0000000001), "AND THE SAME AS A DISTANCE")
    Assert(Close(EngAngleOf(1, 1), Pi() / 4, 0.0000000001), "DIRECTION OF A DIAGONAL")
    Assert(Close(EngRectX(2, 0), 2, 0.0000000001), "RECTANGULAR X")
    Assert(Close(EngRectY(2, Pi() / 2), 2, 0.0000000001), "RECTANGULAR Y")
    Assert(Close(Mag(EngPolarOf(3, 4)), 5, 0.0000000001), "POLAR MAGNITUDE")
    Assert(Close(Theta(EngPolarOf(0, 1)), Pi() / 2, 0.0000000001), "POLAR ANGLE")

    Print("--- HYPERBOLICS ---")
    Assert(Close(EngSinh(0), 0, 0.0000000001), "SINH OF ZERO")
    Assert(Close(EngCosh(0), 1, 0.0000000001), "COSH OF ZERO")
    Assert(Close(EngSinh(1), 1.1752011936, 0.0000000001), "SINH OF ONE")
    Assert(Close(EngCosh(1), 1.5430806348, 0.0000000001), "COSH OF ONE")
    Assert(Close(EngTanh(1), 0.7615941560, 0.0000000001), "TANH OF ONE")
    Rem The defining identity, at an argument where both terms are large.
    Assert(Close(EngCosh(2) * EngCosh(2) - EngSinh(2) * EngSinh(2), 1, 0.0000000001),
           "COSH SQUARED MINUS SINH SQUARED IS ONE")
    Assert(Close(EngSech(0), 1, 0.0000000001), "SECH OF ZERO")
    Assert(Close(EngCsch(1), 0.8509181282, 0.0000000001), "CSCH OF ONE")
    Assert(Close(EngCoth(1), 1.3130352855, 0.0000000001), "COTH OF ONE")
    Assert(Close(EngAsinh(1), 0.8813735870, 0.0000000001), "ARSINH OF ONE")
    Assert(Close(EngAcosh(1), 0, 0.0000000001), "ARCOSH OF ONE IS ZERO")
    Assert(Close(EngAtanh(0.5), 0.5493061443, 0.0000000001), "ARTANH OF A HALF")
    Assert(Close(EngAsech(0.5), 1.3169578969, 0.0000000001), "ARSECH OF A HALF")
    Assert(Close(EngAcsch(1), 0.8813735870, 0.0000000001), "ARCSCH OF ONE")
    Assert(Close(EngAcoth(2), 0.5493061443, 0.0000000001), "ARCOTH OF TWO")

    Print("--- LOGARITHMS AND ROOTS ---")
    Assert(Close(EngLog2(8), 3, 0.0000000001), "LOG BASE 2 OF 8")
    Assert(Close(EngLogBase(10, 1000), 3, 0.0000000001), "LOG BASE 10 OF 1000")
    Assert(Close(EngLog1p(1), 0.6931471806, 0.0000000001), "LOG1P OF ONE IS LOG 2")
    Assert(Close(EngExpm1(1), 1.7182818285, 0.0000000001), "EXPM1 OF ONE IS E MINUS 1")
    Assert(Close(EngExpm1(0), 0, 0.0000000001), "EXPM1 OF ZERO")
    Rem Near zero the naive forms lose their digits; these must not.
    Assert(Close(EngLog1p(0.0000000001) * 10000000000, 1, 0.00001),
           "LOG1P KEEPS ITS DIGITS NEAR ZERO")
    Assert(Close(EngExpm1(0.0000000001) * 10000000000, 1, 0.00001),
           "AND SO DOES EXPM1")
    Assert(Close(EngNthRoot(27, 3), 3, 0.0000000001), "CUBE ROOT OF 27")
    Rem The case x ^ (1/n) gets wrong.
    Assert(Close(EngNthRoot(0 - 27, 3), 0 - 3, 0.0000000001),
           "AND THE CUBE ROOT OF A NEGATIVE")
    Assert(Close(EngNthRoot(16, 4), 2, 0.0000000001), "FOURTH ROOT OF 16")

    Print("--- COMPLEX NUMBERS ---")
    Assert(EngCNear(EngCAdd(EngComplex(1, 2), EngComplex(3, 4)), EngComplex(4, 6),
                    0.0000000001), "COMPLEX ADDITION")
    Assert(EngCNear(EngCSub(EngComplex(1, 2), EngComplex(3, 4)),
                    EngComplex(0 - 2, 0 - 2), 0.0000000001), "COMPLEX SUBTRACTION")
    Rem (1 + 2i)(3 + 4i) = 3 + 4i + 6i + 8i^2 = -5 + 10i.
    Assert(EngCNear(EngCMul(EngComplex(1, 2), EngComplex(3, 4)),
                    EngComplex(0 - 5, 10), 0.0000000001), "COMPLEX MULTIPLICATION")
    Rem (1 + 2i)/(3 + 4i) = (11 + 2i)/25.
    Assert(EngCNear(EngCDiv(EngComplex(1, 2), EngComplex(3, 4)),
                    EngComplex(0.44, 0.08), 0.0000000001), "COMPLEX DIVISION")
    Assert(EngCNear(EngConj(EngComplex(3, 4)), EngComplex(3, 0 - 4), 0.0000000001),
           "CONJUGATE")
    Assert(Close(EngCAbs(EngComplex(3, 4)), 5, 0.0000000001), "MODULUS")
    Assert(Close(EngCArg(EngComplex(1, 1)), Pi() / 4, 0.0000000001), "ARGUMENT")
    Rem Euler: e^(i pi) + 1 = 0.
    Assert(EngCNear(EngCExp(EngComplex(0, Pi())), EngComplex(0 - 1, 0), 0.0000000001),
           "EULERS IDENTITY")
    Assert(EngCNear(EngCSqrt(EngComplex(0 - 1, 0)), EngComplex(0, 1), 0.0000000001),
           "THE SQUARE ROOT OF MINUS ONE")
    Assert(EngCNear(EngCPow(EngComplex(0, 1), 2), EngComplex(0 - 1, 0), 0.0000000001),
           "I SQUARED IS MINUS ONE")
    Assert(EngCNear(EngCLog(EngCExp(EngComplex(0.5, 1))), EngComplex(0.5, 1),
                    0.0000000001), "LOG UNDOES EXP")
    Assert(Close(Mag(EngCToPolar(EngComplex(3, 4))), 5, 0.0000000001),
           "COMPLEX TO POLAR")
    Assert(EngCNear(EngCFromPolar(5, EngCArg(EngComplex(3, 4))), EngComplex(3, 4),
                    0.0000000001), "AND POLAR TO COMPLEX")
    Rem = is never the right comparison here, and this says why.
    Assert((EngCAdd(EngComplex(0.1, 0), EngComplex(0.2, 0)) = EngComplex(0.3, 0)) = False,
           "STRUCTURAL EQUALITY IS WRONG ON COMPLEX NUMBERS")
    Assert(EngCNear(EngCAdd(EngComplex(0.1, 0), EngComplex(0.2, 0)), EngComplex(0.3, 0),
                    0.0000000001), "AND ENGCNEAR IS RIGHT")
    Rem The zero part is dropped and a negative never prints as "+ -".
    Assert(EngCShow(EngComplex(5, 0)) = Str(5), "A REAL SHOWS WITHOUT AN I")
    Assert(EngCShow(EngComplex(0, 2)) = Str(2) & "i", "AN IMAGINARY SHOWS WITHOUT A REAL")
    Assert(EngCShow(EngComplex(3, 0 - 4)) = Str(3) & " - " & Str(4) & "i",
           "AND A NEGATIVE IMAGINARY PART SUBTRACTS")

    Print("--- DESCRIPTIVE STATISTICS ---")
    Assert(Close(EngSum({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }), 10, 0.0000000001), "SUM")
    Assert(Close(EngMean({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }), 2.5, 0.0000000001), "MEAN")
    Assert(Close(EngMedian({ 1, 3, 2 }), 2, 0.0000000001), "MEDIAN OF AN ODD COUNT")
    Assert(Close(EngMedian({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }), 2.5, 0.0000000001), "AND OF AN EVEN COUNT")
    Rem The same eight numbers to both words. Sample divides by n-1 and
    Rem population by n; either wired to either divisor passes a test that
    Rem checks only one.
    Assert(Close(EngStdDev({ 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9 }), 2.1380899353, 0.0000000001),
           "SAMPLE STANDARD DEVIATION")
    Assert(Close(EngStdDevP({ 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9 }), 2, 0.0000000001),
           "POPULATION STANDARD DEVIATION ON THE SAME DATA")
    Assert(Close(EngVar({ 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9 }), 4.5714285714, 0.0000000001),
           "SAMPLE VARIANCE")
    Assert(Close(EngVarP({ 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9 }), 4, 0.0000000001),
           "POPULATION VARIANCE")
    Assert(Close(EngQuantile({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, 0.5), 2.5, 0.0000000001), "THE MEDIAN QUANTILE")
    Assert(Close(EngMinimum({ 3, 1, 2 }), 1, 0.0000000001), "MINIMUM")
    Assert(Close(EngMaximum({ 3, 1, 2 }), 3, 0.0000000001), "MAXIMUM")
    Assert(Close(EngRangeOf({ 1, 5, 3 }), 4, 0.0000000001), "RANGE")
    Assert(Close(EngSkewness({ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "SYMMETRIC DATA HAS NO SKEW")
    Assert(Close(EngGeoMean({ 1, 4, 16 }), 4, 0.0000000001), "GEOMETRIC MEAN")
    Assert(Close(EngWeightedMean({ 1, 2 }, { 1, 3 }), 1.75, 0.0000000001), "WEIGHTED MEAN")
    Assert(Close(EngCorrel({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 2, 4, 6 }), 1, 0.0000000001),
           "A PERFECT LINE CORRELATES AT ONE")
    Assert(Close(EngCov({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 2, 4, 6 }), 2, 0.0000000001), "COVARIANCE")
    Assert(Close(EngSpearman({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 1, 10, 100 }), 1, 0.0000000001),
           "SPEARMAN SEES THE RANKS, NOT THE VALUES")
    Assert(Close(Avg(EngStat1({ 1, 2, 3, 4 })), 2.5, 0.0000000001), "SUMMARY AVERAGE")
    Assert(Close(Sd(EngStat1({ 1, 2, 3, 4 })), 1.2909944487, 0.0000000001), "SUMMARY SD")
    Assert(Close(Total(EngStat1({ 1, 2, 3, 4 })), 10, 0.0000000001), "SUMMARY TOTAL")

    Print("--- DISTRIBUTIONS ---")
    Assert(Close(EngNormPdf(0, 0, 1), 0.3989422804, 0.0000000001), "STANDARD NORMAL PEAK")
    Rem The density scales by 1/sd, which is the part a z-score-only word
    Rem leaves to the caller to forget.
    Assert(Close(EngNormPdf(100, 100, 15), 0.0265961520, 0.0000000001),
           "AND SCALES WITH THE SPREAD")
    Assert(Close(EngNormCdf(0, 0, 1), 0.5, 0.000001), "HALF THE NORMAL IS BELOW THE MEAN")
    Assert(Close(EngNormCdf(1, 0, 1), 0.8413447461, 0.000001), "ONE SIGMA")
    Assert(Close(EngNormCdf(110, 100, 15), 0.7475074625, 0.000001), "AN IQ OF 110")
    Assert(Close(EngNormInv(0.975), 1.9599639845, 0.000001), "THE 97.5TH PERCENTILE")
    Assert(Close(EngTInv(0.975, 10), 2.2281388519, 0.0001), "STUDENT T AT TEN DEGREES")
    Assert(Close(EngTCdf(EngTInv(0.975, 10), 10), 0.975, 0.0001), "AND ITS CDF UNDOES IT")
    Assert(Close(EngChi2Cdf(3.8414588207, 1), 0.95, 0.001), "CHI SQUARED AT ONE DEGREE")
    Assert(Close(EngFCdf(1, 10, 10), 0.5, 0.001), "F IS SYMMETRIC AT ONE WITH EQUAL DEGREES")
    Assert(Close(EngExpCdf(1, 1), 0.6321205588, 0.0000000001), "EXPONENTIAL CDF")

    Print("--- CURVE FITTING ---")
    Rem y = 2x + 1, exactly.
    Assert(Close(Intercept(EngFitOf({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, { 3, 5, 7, 9 }, EngLinear())), 1,
                 0.0000000001), "LINEAR INTERCEPT")
    Assert(Close(Slope(EngFitOf({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, { 3, 5, 7, 9 }, EngLinear())), 2,
                 0.0000000001), "LINEAR SLOPE")
    Assert(Close(RFit(EngFitOf({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, { 3, 5, 7, 9 }, EngLinear())), 1,
                 0.0000000001), "AND A PERFECT FIT")
    Rem y = 3 * 2^x, so the exponential form recovers a = 3 and b = ln 2.
    Assert(Close(Intercept(EngFitOf({ 0, 1, 2, 3 }, { 3, 6, 12, 24 }, EngExpModel())), 3,
                 0.0000000001), "EXPONENTIAL COEFFICIENT")
    Assert(Close(Slope(EngFitOf({ 0, 1, 2, 3 }, { 3, 6, 12, 24 }, EngExpModel())),
                 0.6931471806, 0.0000000001), "EXPONENTIAL RATE IS LOG 2")
    Rem y = 2 x^3.
    Assert(Close(Intercept(EngFitOf({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, { 2, 16, 54, 128 }, EngPowerModel())),
                 2, 0.0000000001), "POWER COEFFICIENT")
    Assert(Close(Slope(EngFitOf({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, { 2, 16, 54, 128 }, EngPowerModel())), 3,
                 0.0000000001), "POWER EXPONENT")
    Rem y = 5 + 2 ln x.
    Assert(Close(Slope(EngFitOf({ 1, 2, 4, 8 },
                                { 5, 6.3862943611, 7.7725887222, 9.1588830834 },
                                EngLogModel())), 2, 0.0000001), "LOGARITHMIC SLOPE")

    Print("--- NUMBER THEORY ---")
    Assert(EngGcd(48, 18) = 6, "GREATEST COMMON DIVISOR")
    Assert(EngGcd(0 - 48, 18) = 6, "AND IT IGNORES SIGN")
    Assert(EngLcm(4, 6) = 12, "LEAST COMMON MULTIPLE")
    Assert(EngIQuot(17, 5) = 3, "INTEGER QUOTIENT")
    Assert(EngIRem(17, 5) = 2, "INTEGER REMAINDER")
    Rem Truncated, not floored: the remainder takes the sign of the
    Rem dividend, which is what Fix gives and what C gives.
    Assert(EngIRem(0 - 17, 5) = 0 - 2, "AND A NEGATIVE DIVIDEND TRUNCATES TOWARD ZERO")
    Assert(EngModPow(2, 10, 1000) = 24, "MODULAR EXPONENTIATION")
    Rem 3^200 has 96 digits. Only square-and-multiply gets here at all.
    Assert(EngModPow(3, 200, 50) = 1, "AND AN EXPONENT NO DIRECT POWER COULD REACH")
    Assert(EngModInv(3, 11) = 4, "MODULAR INVERSE")
    Assert(EngIsPrime(97), "97 IS PRIME")
    Assert(Not EngIsPrime(91), "91 IS 7 TIMES 13")
    Assert(Not EngIsPrime(1), "AND ONE IS NOT PRIME")
    Assert(Length(EngPrimes(20)) = 8, "EIGHT PRIMES BELOW 20")
    Assert(EngSum(EngPrimes(20)) = 77, "AND THEY SUM TO 77")
    Rem 360 = 2 * 2 * 2 * 3 * 3 * 5.
    Assert(Length(EngFactorize(360)) = 6, "SIX PRIME FACTORS OF 360")
    Assert(EngSum(EngFactorize(360)) = 17, "AND THEY SUM TO 17")
    Assert(Length(EngFactorize(97)) = 1, "A PRIME HAS ONE FACTOR")
    Assert(Length(EngDivisors(28)) = 6, "SIX DIVISORS OF 28")
    Rem 28 is perfect: its divisors sum to twice itself.
    Assert(EngSum(EngDivisors(28)) = 56, "AND 28 IS A PERFECT NUMBER")
    Assert(EngTotient(36) = 12, "EULERS TOTIENT")
    Assert(EngTotient(97) = 96, "AND A PRIME LEAVES ALL BUT ITSELF")

    Print("--- COMBINATORICS ---")
    Assert(EngFact(0) = 1, "ZERO FACTORIAL IS ONE")
    Assert(EngFact(10) = 3628800, "TEN FACTORIAL")
    Assert(EngFact(18) = 6402373705728000, "AND 18! IS THE LAST EXACT ONE")
    Assert(EngComb(5, 2) = 10, "FIVE CHOOSE TWO")
    Rem Exactly, not nearly: the factorial form passes 2^53 en route.
    Assert(EngComb(52, 5) = 2598960, "AND A POKER HAND IS EXACTLY 2598960")
    Assert(EngComb(5, 0) = 1, "CHOOSING NOTHING")
    Assert(EngComb(5, 6) = 0, "AND CHOOSING MORE THAN THERE IS")
    Assert(EngPerm(5, 2) = 20, "FIVE PERMUTE TWO")
    Assert(EngPerm(10, 3) = 720, "AND TEN PERMUTE THREE")
    Assert(EngFib(0) = 0, "THE FIRST FIBONACCI")
    Assert(EngFib(10) = 55, "AND THE TENTH")
    Assert(Close(EngGamma(5), 24, 0.0000001), "GAMMA OF 5 IS 4 FACTORIAL")
    Rem Gamma(1/2) is the square root of pi - the value that shows the
    Rem function is not just factorials at the whole numbers.
    Assert(Close(EngGamma(0.5), 1.7724538509, 0.0000001), "GAMMA OF A HALF IS ROOT PI")
    Assert(Close(EngGammaLn(10), 12.8018274801, 0.0000001), "LOG GAMMA OF 10")
    Assert(Close(EngBeta(2, 3), 0.0833333333, 0.0000001), "BETA OF 2 AND 3 IS A TWELFTH")

    Print("--- DISCRETE DISTRIBUTIONS ---")
    Assert(Close(EngBinomPdf(2, 5, 0.5), 0.3125, 0.0000000001), "TWO HEADS IN FIVE TOSSES")
    Assert(Close(EngBinomCdf(2, 5, 0.5), 0.5, 0.0000000001), "AT MOST TWO IS EVEN MONEY")
    Assert(Close(EngBinomCdf(5, 5, 0.5), 1, 0.0000000001), "AND THE WHOLE MASS IS ONE")
    Assert(Close(EngPoissonPdf(0, 2), 0.1353352832, 0.0000000001), "NO ARRIVALS")
    Assert(Close(EngPoissonPdf(2, 3), 0.2240418077, 0.0000000001), "TWO ARRIVALS AT RATE 3")
    Assert(Close(EngPoissonCdf(1, 1), 0.7357588823, 0.0000000001), "AT MOST ONE AT RATE 1")
    Assert(Close(EngGeomPdf(3, 0.5), 0.125, 0.0000000001), "FIRST SUCCESS ON THE THIRD TRY")
    Assert(Close(EngGeomCdf(3, 0.5), 0.875, 0.0000000001), "WITHIN THREE TRIES")
    Rem Two aces in a five-card hand: C(4,2) C(48,3) / C(52,5).
    Assert(Close(EngHyperPdf(2, 5, 4, 52), 0.0399298181, 0.0000000001),
           "TWO ACES IN A POKER HAND")
    Assert(Close(EngHyperPdf(5, 5, 4, 52), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND FIVE ACES IS IMPOSSIBLE")

    Print("--- NUMERIC CALCULUS ---")
    Assert(Close(EngDeriv(Fn(x) => x * x, 3), 6, 0.0000001), "THE DERIVATIVE OF X SQUARED")
    Assert(Close(EngDeriv(Fn(x) => Sin(x), 0), 1, 0.0000001), "COS ZERO FROM SIN")
    Assert(Close(EngDeriv2(Fn(x) => x * x * x, 2), 12, 0.0001), "THE SECOND DERIVATIVE OF A CUBE")
    Rem Simpson is exact for cubics and below, so this must be exact.
    Assert(Close(EngIntegrate(Fn(x) => x * x, 0, 1, 100), 0.3333333333, 0.0000000001),
           "THE AREA UNDER X SQUARED")
    Assert(Close(EngIntegrate(Fn(x) => Sin(x), 0, Pi(), 100), 2, 0.0000001),
           "THE AREA UNDER A HALF SINE")
    Rem n = 2 is the smallest legal interval count and takes the weighting
    Rem path with no interior 2s at all.
    Assert(Close(EngIntegrate(Fn(x) => x * x, 0, 1, 2), 0.3333333333, 0.0000000001),
           "AND TWO INTERVALS IS ENOUGH FOR A PARABOLA")
    Assert(EngSumOf(Fn(i) => i, 1, 100) = 5050, "THE SUM GAUSS DID AT SCHOOL")
    Assert(EngProdOf(Fn(i) => i, 1, 5) = 120, "AND A PRODUCT IS A FACTORIAL")

    Print("--- ROOTS AND OPTIMISATION ---")
    Assert(Close(EngZeroOf(Fn(x) => x * x - 2, 0, 2), 1.4142135624, 0.0000000001),
           "BISECTION FINDS ROOT TWO")
    Assert(Close(EngNewtonOf(Fn(x) => x * x - 2, 1), 1.4142135624, 0.0000000001),
           "AND SO DOES NEWTON")
    Assert(Close(EngZeroOf(Fn(x) => Cos(x) - x, 0, 1), 0.7390851332, 0.0000000001),
           "THE DOTTIE NUMBER")
    Assert(Close(EngMinOf(Fn(x) => (x - 3) * (x - 3) + 1, 0, 10), 3, 0.0000001),
           "THE MINIMUM OF A PARABOLA")
    Assert(Close(EngMaxOf(Fn(x) => 0 - (x - 3) * (x - 3), 0, 10), 3, 0.0000001),
           "AND THE MAXIMUM OF ITS REFLECTION")

    Print("--- POLYNOMIALS ---")
    Rem { 1, 0, -4 } is x^2 - 4, highest power first.
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval({ 1, 0, 0 - 4 }, 3), 5, 0.0000000001), "HORNER EVALUATION")
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval({ 1, 0, 0 - 4 }, 2), 0, 0.0000000001), "AND TWO IS A ROOT")
    Assert(EngPolyDegree({ 0, 0, 1, 2 }) = 1, "LEADING ZEROS DO NOT COUNT AS DEGREE")
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval(EngPolyAdd({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 4, 5 }), 2), 24, 0.0000000001),
           "ADDITION ALIGNS ON THE LOWEST POWER")
    Rem (x^2 + 2x + 3) - (2x + 3) = x^2, which is 4 at x = 2.
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval(EngPolySub({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 2, 3 }), 2), 4, 0.0000000001),
           "SUBTRACTION")
    Rem And when the leading term cancels, the degree must drop with it.
    Assert(Length(EngPolySub({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 1, 0, 0 })) = 2, "A CANCELLED LEADING TERM TRIMS")
    Rem (x + 1)(x - 1) = x^2 - 1, which is 3 at x = 2.
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval(EngPolyMul({ 1, 1 }, { 1, 0 - 1 }), 2), 3, 0.0000000001),
           "MULTIPLICATION")
    Assert(Length(EngPolyMul({ 1, 1 }, { 1, 0 - 1 })) = 3, "AND THE DEGREES ADD")
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval(EngPolyScale({ 1, 2 }, 3), 1), 9, 0.0000000001), "SCALING")
    Rem (x^2 - 4) / (x - 2) = x + 2, remainder 0.
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval(Quotient(EngPolyDiv({ 1, 0, 0 - 4 }, { 1, 0 - 2 })), 1), 3,
                 0.0000000001), "DIVISION QUOTIENT")
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval(Remainder(EngPolyDiv({ 1, 0, 0 - 4 }, { 1, 0 - 2 })), 7), 0,
                 0.0000000001), "AND AN EXACT DIVISION LEAVES NOTHING")
    Rem (x^2 + 1) / (x - 1) = x + 1, remainder 2.
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval(Remainder(EngPolyDiv({ 1, 0, 1 }, { 1, 0 - 1 })), 7), 2,
                 0.0000000001), "AND AN INEXACT ONE LEAVES A REMAINDER")
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval(EngPolyDeriv({ 1, 0, 0 - 4 }), 3), 6, 0.0000000001),
           "DIFFERENTIATION")
    Assert(Close(EngPolyEval(EngPolyIntegral({ 2, 0 }, 0), 3), 9, 0.0000000001),
           "INTEGRATION")
    Assert(Close(EngPolyArea({ 3, 0, 0 }, 0, 2), 8, 0.0000000001), "DEFINITE AREA")
    Rem x^2 - 4 has real roots at 2 and -2.
    Assert(Close(Re(Nth(EngQuadRoots(1, 0, 0 - 4), 1)), 2, 0.0000000001), "A REAL ROOT")
    Assert(Close(Im(Nth(EngQuadRoots(1, 0, 0 - 4), 1)), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "WITH NO IMAGINARY PART")
    Rem x^2 + 1 has roots at +i and -i, and the word returns complex either
    Rem way rather than changing its return type.
    Assert(Close(Im(Nth(EngQuadRoots(1, 0, 1), 1)), 1, 0.0000000001), "AND A COMPLEX ROOT")
    Assert(Close(Re(Nth(EngQuadRoots(1, 0, 1), 1)), 0, 0.0000000001), "WITH NO REAL PART")
    Rem (x-1)(x-2)(x-3) = x^3 - 6x^2 + 11x - 6. Three real roots, so this
    Rem goes through the trigonometric form.
    Assert(Length(EngCubicReal(1, 0 - 6, 11, 0 - 6)) = 3, "THREE REAL CUBIC ROOTS")
    Assert(Close(EngSum(EngCubicReal(1, 0 - 6, 11, 0 - 6)), 6, 0.0000001),
           "AND THEY SUM TO SIX")
    Rem x^3 - 8 has one real root, reached through Cardano's radicals
    Rem instead - no arithmetic shared with the branch above.
    Assert(Length(EngCubicReal(1, 0, 0, 0 - 8)) = 1, "ONE REAL CUBIC ROOT")
    Assert(Close(Nth(EngCubicReal(1, 0, 0, 0 - 8), 1), 2, 0.0000001), "AND IT IS TWO")

    Print("--- SEQUENCES ---")
    Assert(Length(EngLinSpace(0, 1, 5)) = 5, "LINSPACE LENGTH")
    Assert(Close(EngSum(EngLinSpace(0, 1, 5)), 2.5, 0.0000000001), "LINSPACE VALUES")
    Assert(Close(Nth(EngLinSpace(0, 1, 5), 5), 1, 0.0000000001),
           "AND IT REACHES THE FAR END EXACTLY")
    Assert(Close(EngSum(EngGeomSpace(1, 1000, 4)), 1111, 0.0000001), "GEOMETRIC SPACING")
    Assert(Close(Nth(EngCumSum({ 1, 2, 3 }), 3), 6, 0.0000000001), "RUNNING TOTAL")
    Assert(Length(EngDiff({ 1, 4, 9, 16 })) = 3, "DIFFERENCES ARE ONE SHORTER")
    Assert(Close(EngSum(EngDiff({ 1, 4, 9, 16 })), 15, 0.0000000001), "AND THEY TELESCOPE")
    Assert(Length(EngMovAvg({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, 2)) = 3, "MOVING AVERAGE LENGTH")
    Assert(Close(EngSum(EngMovAvg({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, 2)), 7.5, 0.0000000001),
           "MOVING AVERAGE VALUES")
    Assert(Close(EngSum(EngNormalize({ 10, 20, 30 })), 1.5, 0.0000000001), "NORMALISE")
    Rem A flat list has a zero range, and the obvious code divides by it.
    Assert(Close(EngSum(EngNormalize({ 5, 5, 5 })), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND A FLAT LIST DOES NOT DIVIDE BY ZERO")
    Assert(Close(EngDotOf({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 4, 5, 6 }), 32, 0.0000000001), "DOT PRODUCT")
    Assert(Close(EngMagOf({ 3, 4 }), 5, 0.0000000001), "VECTOR MAGNITUDE")

    Print("--- UNITS ---")
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(1, EngMetre(), EngCentimetre()), 100, 0.0000000001),
           "METRES TO CENTIMETRES")
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(1, EngMile(), EngKilometre()), 1.609344, 0.0000000001),
           "MILES TO KILOMETRES")
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(12, EngInch(), EngFoot()), 1, 0.0000000001), "INCHES TO FEET")
    Rem Fourteen pounds to the stone, as the mill records had it.
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(1, EngStone(), EngPound()), 14, 0.0000001), "STONES TO POUNDS")
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(1, EngHour(), EngMinute()), 60, 0.0000000001), "HOURS TO MINUTES")
    Rem Temperature is the only quantity with an offset, and both fixed
    Rem points must land.
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(100, EngCelsius(), EngFahrenheit()), 212, 0.0000001),
           "BOILING POINT")
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(32, EngFahrenheit(), EngCelsius()), 0, 0.0000001),
           "AND FREEZING POINT")
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(0, EngCelsius(), EngKelvin()), 273.15, 0.0000001),
           "AND ABSOLUTE ZERO IS THE OFFSET")
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(1, EngAtm(), EngPsi()), 14.6959487755, 0.0000001),
           "ATMOSPHERES TO PSI")
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(1, EngKwh(), EngJoule()), 3600000, 0.0000001),
           "KILOWATT HOURS TO JOULES")
    Assert(Close(EngConvert(1, EngKph(), EngMps()), 0.2777777778, 0.0000001),
           "KILOMETRES PER HOUR TO METRES PER SECOND")
    Assert(Quantity(EngMetre()) = "LENGTH", "A UNIT CARRIES ITS QUANTITY")
    Assert(Name(EngPsi()) = "PSI", "AND ITS NAME")

    Print("--- CONSTANTS ---")
    Assert(Value(EngLightSpeed()) = 299792458, "THE SPEED OF LIGHT IS EXACT BY DEFINITION")
    Assert(Units(EngLightSpeed()) = "M/S", "AND CARRIES ITS UNITS")
    Assert(CName(EngLightSpeed()) = "SPEED OF LIGHT", "AND ITS NAME")
    Assert(Close(Value(EngAvogadro()) / (10 ^ 23), 6.02214076, 0.0000001),
           "AVOGADRO CONSTANT")
    Assert(Close(Value(EngPlanck()) * (10 ^ 34), 6.62607015, 0.0000001), "PLANCK CONSTANT")
    Assert(Close(Value(EngStdGravity()), 9.80665, 0.0000001), "STANDARD GRAVITY")
    Assert(Close(Value(EngGasConstant()), 8.314462618, 0.0000001), "MOLAR GAS CONSTANT")
    Assert(Close(EngE(), 2.7182818285, 0.0000000001), "E")
    Assert(Close(EngTau(), 6.2831853072, 0.0000000001), "TAU IS TWO PI")

    Print("--- PRESENTATION ---")
    Assert(Close(EngRoundTo(3.14159, 2), 3.14, 0.0000000001), "ROUNDING TO PLACES")
    Assert(Close(EngFracOf(2.75), 0.75, 0.0000000001), "FRACTIONAL PART")
    Assert(Close(EngClamp(5, 0, 3), 3, 0.0000000001), "CLAMPING")
    Assert(Close(EngLerp(0, 10, 0.25), 2.5, 0.0000000001), "INTERPOLATION")
    Assert(Close(EngInvLerp(0, 10, 2.5), 0.25, 0.0000000001), "AND BACK AGAIN")
    Assert(Close(EngRemap(5, 0, 10, 0, 100), 50, 0.0000000001), "REMAPPING")
    Assert(Close(EngSmoothstep(0, 1, 0.5), 0.5, 0.0000000001), "SMOOTHSTEP AT THE MIDPOINT")
    Assert(EngSign(0 - 5) = 0 - 1, "SIGN OF A NEGATIVE")
    Assert(EngSign(0) = 0, "AND OF ZERO")
    Assert(EngNear(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3, 0.0000000001), "TOLERANT COMPARISON")
    Rem Significant figures, not decimal places - the distinction the two
    Rem words exist to keep apart.
    Assert(Close(EngSigFig(0.00123456, 3), 0.00123, 0.0000000001), "THREE SIGNIFICANT FIGURES")
    Assert(Close(EngSigFig(123456, 3), 123000, 0.0000000001), "AND THREE OF A LARGE NUMBER")
    Assert(Close(EngRoundTo(0.00123456, 3), 0.001, 0.0000000001),
           "WHERE ROUNDING TO PLACES GIVES SOMETHING ELSE")
    Assert(EngSigFig(0, 3) = 0, "AND ZERO HAS NO EXPONENT TO TAKE")
    Assert(EngMetricPrefix(0) = "", "THE UNPREFIXED DECADE")
    Assert(EngMetricPrefix(1) = "k", "KILO")
    Assert(EngMetricPrefix(0 - 2) = "u", "AND MICRO")
    Rem 47000 ohms is written 47k on the part.
    Assert(EngMetric(47000, 0) = "47k", "ENGINEERING NOTATION")
    Assert(EngMetric(0.0000022, 1) = "2.2u", "AND BELOW ONE")
    Assert(EngSci(12345, 2) = "1.23E+4", "SCIENTIFIC NOTATION")
    Assert(EngSci(0.00012345, 2) = "1.23E-4", "AND A NEGATIVE EXPONENT")
    Assert(EngCommas(1234567, 0) = "1,234,567", "GROUPED DIGITS")

    Print("--- the tables are one value, not one per call ---")
    Rem The prefix list compares by identity, so this was False while a Def
    Rem rebuilt it; the unit and constant records are now built once rather
    Rem than twice per EngConvert.
    Assert(EngPrefixes() = EngPrefixes(), "ENGPREFIXES IS THE SAME LIST EVERY CALL")
    Assert(EngMile() = EngMile(), "AND A UNIT IS THE SAME RECORD")
    Assert(Factor(EngKnot()) = 1852 / 3600, "ARITHMETIC IN A CONSTANT'S ARGUMENTS FOLDED EXACTLY")
    Assert(Value(EngPlanck()) = 6.62607015 * (10 ^ (0 - 34)), "AND THE CODATA MANTISSA TIMES ITS POWER OF TEN")

    Print("")
    Print("ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED")
